A||||30||FieroKyle||Fiero V-8||||1||
Z||000000||FieroKyle||06-21-2001||01:36 AM||||Hi, I was considering to put a V-8 sbc in my fiero. I was wondering if it was worth the time and money to really have that 350 h.p. flowing through the little guy, I am afraid the engine with have costly maintance and cause abnormal abuse to my fiero with all that extra power and the little more wieght. Thanks for anyone who makes suggestions on the V-8 engine, or any other mods to make my Fiero get more horsepower and torque. Also I would like know if those hyperchip performance computer chip really make a differnce in your fiero's engine performance.<BR>Thanks. =)<P>Kyle - 85' GT ||||reg||1
Z||000001||KRMFiero||06-21-2001||10:32 AM||||if you want to go SBC go to <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com" TARGET=_blank>www.v8archie.com</A>  if you havent been there already, he can and will answer any question you have...<P>Another V-8 you could get, it doesnt make as much HP, makes good torqe, still sounds the same but does not require a kit ot put in (well you have to buy mounts or make them) is the Cadilliac 4.x series engines, I am currently in the proccess of the 4.5 with its 440T 4 speed auto tranny going into my 84. here is the site of the guy how specalises in this, his name is Joe: <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx</A> <BR>he can answer any questions about his swap, that engine going into other cars, he can answer quersions about other swaps very smart person when it comes to BOP engines...<P><BR>Kyle<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (Red) Caddy 4.5 Project<BR>1985 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (White)<P>      <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/sig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bazooka||06-21-2001||10:33 AM||||Kyle,  You can boost your power more directly with mods to your existing 2.8 like porting your Intake, Plenum and Throttle Body, and Exhaust Manifold. Also look into the 1.6 Ratio Rocker Arms and a 3 angle valve job. MSD Ignition upgrade would also help. Pass on the Hypertech Chip as reports have shown no difference in performance.<BR>   As for the SBC conversion, I wouldn't waste any money on it as I have had one done that was the biggest waste and it was done by a popular shop. The maintenance requires constant special attention and risks as most shops will point out that the high torque of a SBC wasn't made for the Fiero Transmissions. The cooling system access and operation is inferior, The exhaust system needs great modification so that it wont choke the engine, and you also need to beef up everything from the wheel studs to the suspension and wiring so that you don't break. Do a Northstar or 3800 SC to enjoy the power and get a matched driveline thats been engineered by GM. <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html" TARGET=_blank>http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html</A> <BR>***************************************<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroKyle:<BR><B>Hi, I was considering to put a V-8 sbc in my fiero. I was wondering if it was worth the time and money to really have that 350 h.p. flowing through the little guy, I am afraid the engine with have costly maintance and cause abnormal abuse to my fiero with all that extra power and the little more wieght. Thanks for anyone who makes suggestions on the V-8 engine, or any other mods to make my Fiero get more horsepower and torque. Also I would like know if those hyperchip performance computer chip really make a differnce in your fiero's engine performance.<BR>Thanks. =)<BR>Kyle - 85' GT </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||BV MotorSports||06-21-2001||11:27 AM||||I agree 100% with Bazooka. Most people only want a v8 for bragging rights. If its going to be a car you drive....it will be a head ache at the least. I have done several v8 swaps (SBC) and I was NEVER happy. I am harder on my self than most people so it may be fine for you. I look at it this way...would I let my wife and son go on a long trip in a v8 Fiero? Nope. 3.8 Fiero? Yep!<BR>Go for a 3.8 supercharged swap. 245hp and 280-90 ftlbs. Thats a 13sec in the 1/4 Fiero BONE STOCK! Plus even the "hot rod" mags call the 3.8 the "small block chevy of the 90's" there are tons of hi-perf parts for them. Its pretty simple to get 300hp out of the 3.8sc.<P>Choice is yours. Spend wisely.<P>Steven<P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 93mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||4
Z||000004||Chris McDonald||06-21-2001||11:28 AM||||It depends on what you want out of the car.  You have to realize that it takes more than a high output motor to have a truly high output car.  The previous comments are fair to say, but it all depends on what you want out of the car.<P>If this is going to be a weekend play toy and a once in a while daily driver, you will have a ball with the V8 conversion.  It will get attention everywhere you go and capture the imagination of even the most conservative people.  There is a trade off in gas mileage, driveability, failure ratings, and the amount of time you will spend putting something that does not belong in the car.<P>On the other hand, if you are after real solid performance and have dreams of out running Vipers and new Corvettes, you might want to come up with something different.  Oh, you will be able to run with some of the fastest cars around, but not for long before something breaks.  <P>Good luck on the decision.  You will also need to have as much as $10,000 to blow if you want to do it right.  That is a quality engine, conversion parts, a/c, detail items, suspension upgrades, and the final touches to the exhaust, custom built parts, and a sweet set of wheels to top it all off.<P>The wild thing is, I know for a fact there is at least one super nice V8 Fiero that you can buy for less than the $10,000 price tag it will take to build one.<P>check out  BBBad's V8 Fiero for sale.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Hartz||06-21-2001||12:50 PM||||If you're looking to buy, BBAD's V8 Fiero is gorgeous - no question.  Also, there was one on eBay the other day (don't know when the listing was scheduled to end, there are two right now on <A HREF="http://www.autotrader.com," TARGET=_blank>www.autotrader.com,</A>  and one on the Michigan Fiero Club board.  <P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||1
Z||000006||TRiAD||06-21-2001||01:00 PM||||Where were all the people slamming SBCs when I asked what V8 to do? I was FLOODED with people saying to do the SBC, and I insisted it was an inferior swap. <P>Look into an LT1, (which is the only V8 Archie himself will put into his OWN car, for future projects) and it's a whole different ballgame. <P>Yes, it's a bit more money, but it's a completely different swap...you're getting a Corvett motor, instead of a truck motor...that alone should tell you something about the performance. <P>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Chris McDonald||06-21-2001||01:49 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR>[B]Where were all the people slamming SBCs when I asked what V8 to do? I was FLOODED with people saying to do the SBC, and I insisted it was an inferior swap. <P>***No Worries***<P>You were talking about the +/- of the N* etc. on the V8 conversion.  Based upon that, most people will suggest the SBC based on available aftermarket parts, assembly cost, etc. etc. etc.  <P>Plus, the SBC can easily be hooked to a manual trans unlike the N*.  <P>The LT-1 is cool.  I have wired many of them in different kinds of cars.  However, for the Fiero, it is a costly way to get a 300 hsp V8.  However, the LT-1 makes for a nicer looking package.<P>You know what opinions are like---we all have one.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Dave Gunsul||06-21-2001||02:38 PM||||I really don't know what some of you guys are talking about. I've seen several V8 Fieros that havn't had any problems that<BR>have been together for years!<BR>Paul Vargyas's is a very good example,<BR>he drives that thing thousands of miles every year to many shows and the thing runs like a top.<BR>What about the pruple pavment eater??<BR>When Rich was alive he drove that thing<BR>to hell and back without any problems either and that one has 450+ HP!<BR>As for the Fiero not being built to handle the power: do any of you really believe that<BR>it's some kind of big coincidence that the V8 fits in the Fiero so nicely?<BR>Pontiac was experimenting with a V8 from<BR>the beginning.<BR>The Fieros chassis is MUCH more solid than<BR>a Camaro/Firebirds chassis and they have had<BR>V8's since the beginning.<BR>Don't believe me, take a ride in a third gen. f-body with a TPI 350 and tell me they<BR>can handle the same power as a Fiero can.<BR>You can just feel how "soft" the chassis is<BR>in an f-body compared to a Fiero.<BR>In a convertable f-body you can actually watch the dash move when you make a turn.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>Just because it's small doesn't mean it's weak.<BR>This is not intended as a flame guys but It<BR>really gets to me when people short change the Fiero. Sorry for the length too.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver<p>[This message has been edited by Dave Gunsul (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||elfiero||06-21-2001||03:16 PM||||I've got a TPI-SBC and I don't have any <BR>problems with breakdowns.I did'nt even buy a<BR>kit when I put it together.Sure I had some<BR>"issuses" when I first hit the road,but nothing I did'nt expect.Now all the accesorys<BR>work right,it does'nt overheat and I would<BR>not hesitate to take it across the country!<BR>just like the highpreist of V8s(archie)says:<BR>any car will break if you whup it enough!||||reg||14
Z||000010||Hartz||06-21-2001||04:42 PM||||In my opinion, elfiero and Dave are right - it truly depends on how you build it.  Any major engine work is going to have its share of hiccups.  Plan for those, don't take shortcuts, and you'll do fine.  I can't tell you how many people I have seen trying to save every last dollar on a V8 swap.  Hey, I'm not rich either, but if you're not going to upgrade to a four-core radiator, and your car then overheats, well, don't be surprised.  If you buy a cheap clutch and it slips, well... you start to get my point.<P>Of my two cars, I'd drive the V8 anywhere, but then, it was done "right."  My turbo still has some sorting to be done.  Lesson learned for me.  <P>I think any of the above mentioned engine options are pretty good swaps depending on what you want when you're done and where your particular skills are at (electric, mechanical, etc.) But make a good solid plan before you start and don't skimp on stuff.<P>Just my $.02.<P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Jay||06-21-2001||06:49 PM||||I agree, I've had my N* for two years now.  Take it to the drag stip and drive it regularly.  Haven't had any problems except the starter.  Changed it in an hour.  It all depends on how you build it.  Do it right or don't do it at all.  I  did the conversion basically by myself, just take your time.  BTW my N* is for sale if the price is right.<P>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||FieroKyle||06-22-2001||01:32 AM||||I know that the V-8 swap is very costly at around 10k, I if I had a v-8 put in it, I would drive down to Archie's myself but I would like to avoid the V-8 if possible and stay with the stock motor without a turbo. But I worry with more horsepower to the current motor, I feel like that will just break down on me alot. If i did get a V-8, it would be a 355 HO with the TPI. I feel like my fiero has plenty of power as of current, but it doesn't have the edge to keep with the higher performance car. I was very disapointed out on a sarturday night looking for trouble (lol), uh i got smoked by a mid 80's Mustang with the 5.0, a new monte carlo SS, and a new Lexus (but that was on the part I didn't know I was dragging till the green went off, lol, I kept up to him once I got going). However I did give a whooping to a 88' cavilier Z24 (i think its that) and a Toyota Celica. I want the more power not just for bragging rights more than I want a sleeper on the road. I want the hps and the torque without:<P>1. cutting my car all up or wrecking its orignality (except I could careless of the orginality if it meant I had to put a V-8)<BR>2. A fix it all the time car pouring hundreds and thousands just to keep the car healthy and running great.<BR>3. Other than putting another engine or hopping up the current engine would ruin anything that is stock on the car or wreck its self. Like turbos tend to use up engines quickly and burning them out. <P>I am just after a faster car without pouring money into a name like Corvette or Porshe even though they have the performance. I just want my road rocket with more power without the headaches. I am just trying to get steered in the right dirrection on what to do. Many people have told me just leave it like it is and keep the car's integrity. So I would just like opinions and options. Couple things I am not gonna do for sure it one, a bigger V-6 (i don't understand the point of that is) or a turbo or anything else. Thanks for the suggestions.<P>Kyle - 85' GT (hopefully soon a very very fast 85' GT, lol haha)<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||elfiero||06-22-2001||08:35 AM||||Kyle,these fieros will NEVER be worth anything as a collector peice-there were <BR>over 300,000 made,and there are still like<BR>30,000 registered.New,a loaded '88GT cost<BR>about 17 grand- today,that SAME CAR with 200mi<BR>is worth about 13 grand-that is a lousy way<BR>to make money!If you put that same 17 grand <BR>in a savings account,today it would be worth<BR>more like 25 grand.So,chop that baby up and<BR>lets see you put in an LT-1 or a TPI383 you<BR>will never regret it.P.S.-if you can't afford it now,you will never afford it later||||reg||6
Z||000014||Butter||06-22-2001||11:09 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by elfiero:<BR><B><BR>any car will break if you whup it enough!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>The Key is above.  TLC goes a long.... way.<BR>So let's Whup up some TLC!<P>My DAILY driver has a 283 sbc and I drive the $hit out of it DAILY.<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000015||Slammed Fiero||06-22-2001||11:23 AM||||I think a problem with a lot of cars on the road that have been modified is that people build up a killer engine tranny combo , rush to put it in and overlook basic maiteance items<P>faulty coolant systems<BR>Electric systems that look like a bowl of spagetti. Things put in place with coat hangers<P>If you have to scrouge the cash to build a V8 fiero , and your car already needs $1000's in work. Don't do it .Get the car mechanically sound before you go and wedge a big motor in it, or during the engine conversion fix all those nagging problems Nothing will magnify problems like a ton of power in car with mechanical problems. <P>Hence forth I'm making sure everything is up to par before I do an engine swap. <P><BR>Just my 2 cents. This pretty much relates to every car project. I have friends with early 80's wanna be muscle cars (monte's camaro's ect). they spend 1000's on motors , and put them into cars that have rust all over them , rigged shift linkages , rads held in place with coat hangers. Little weiner 8 bolt axles  trying to stand up to the punishment of a 454 crate motor... Rather funny , everytime they get on the gas..something snaps, breaks , comes loose , leaks. Even seen a motor mount break on a big block? . Do it just right and the motor will flop on it's side , the home made carb linkage will lock at full throttle and before you can say anything the car takes off into the intersection rearending a cop.<P>the cop was not impressed( his car was a write off), thankfully no one was hurt , and I was just glad to ge the hell out of the car, in one piece.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">      <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals. &lt;b&gt; New White Face Gauges , 75Hp Shot of NOS&lt;/b&gt;Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000016||RDosdorian||06-22-2001||01:37 PM||||I just wanted to give my opinion(s) on this subject.  I currently have an LT1 in my 88GT.  The motor came from a 1995 Corvette.  I sent the car and motor to Archie to have the conversion done.  With anything that you modify, you are bound to have some issues.  However, you need to expect that and come to terms with it before you begin something like this.  <P>Horsepower…  My LT1 is still in its stock form.  It is rated at 300hp and that is plenty for the Fiero.  I used to say that you can never have too much horsepower.  Now I am thinking that I may have been wrong when it comes to this car.  I can literally get the front wheels off the ground if I do a “drag strip” launch in it. Yes, I can roast the tires, but due to the placement of the motor, the car hooks up better than I had ever expected.  1.8 60’ times at the drag strip show that fact.  I could not imagine needing any more power.  The car is almost to the point that it is not enjoyable to drive.  Now I don’t want anyone to quote me and give me a hard time for that last statement.  All I am saying is the car is harder to drive in its current form.  The power comes on very hard and quick.  It is considerably noisier (and I am running two cats and a muffler), and I get more vibration than I thought there would be.<P>The AWWW! Factor!?  You open the rear deck lid and people flock to the car to drool.  You get comments like “How did you get that in there?”  “It fits??”  “Oh sh%&, (the guy turns to his friend) look at this.”  Those of us who have these conversions have heard these types of comments and many more.  This is the part I like.<P>Problems…  I, personally, have not had overheating issues.  My problem is the clutch.  Even with Archies clutch (and yes I have spoken to him about it.)  the clutch slips under ¼ mile racing conditions.  It stays hooked up until I go from 3rd to 4th at high rpm’s.  Upon entering 4th, the clutch just slips.  California Smog is another issue.  But, that just upsets me and I won’t get into that here.<P>As for the Northstar conversion.  I think this is a great conversion as well, however, I think the sbc looks better in the engine compartment than the Northstar.  The Fuel delivery system has more character and it dresses up nicer (chrome, color, etc.)  Everything on the Northstar is covered up.  Again, no disrespect to those that went that way.  This is nothing more than a personal opinion.<P>Lastly, it is expensive.  Then again, building a car “the right way” is always that way.  You come up with a budget, then you end up over that.  Something always comes up.  Sometimes it is a major problem and other times you may just wants something powder coated that you didn’t think about when you were coming up with your budget.  <P>Ray <BR>1987 GT (Daily Driver)<BR>1988 GT (LT1)<BR>1988 GT (3,229 miles)<P><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.goldengatefieros.org/RDosdorian_sig.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by RDosdorian (edited 06-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Slammed Fiero||06-22-2001||02:11 PM||||Did archie say anything could be done about the clutch?? what about a ceramic unit or soemthing of that nature.<P>Got any pics of your car? <BR>1/4 mile times??<P>Sounds like you have a really nice setup. How long have you had it and how many miles have you put on it <P>Thanks!<BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">      <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals. &lt;b&gt; New White Face Gauges , 75Hp Shot of NOS&lt;/b&gt;Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Dave Gunsul||06-22-2001||02:12 PM||||Kyle my GT took a Cobra with just bolt ons done to the V6! (it was a 4.6 convert. and<BR>the guy couldn't drive to save his own life but still.)<BR>I'm in the process of getting my new V6 into<BR>the car (God will it ever be done??) and I<BR>can tell you that the V6 can produce some<BR>impressive power!<BR>Most people, myself included, go with a bigger V6 because that gives you more torque<BR>without adding any weight. Most Fiero's<BR>are tipping or already over the 100,000 mile<BR>mark so a rebuild is needed anyway.<BR>Why don't you start adding some bolt on items<BR>to your currrent engine and then start a rebuild project on a V6 with some internal mods (like cam and higher comp. pistons) over<BR>the winter? The Fiero store sells everything<BR>you'll need for a pretty stout 2.8.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000019||RDosdorian||06-22-2001||02:23 PM||||Slammed....Archie is waiting for me to pull the clutch to see what happend.  I am looking into other clutch options.  Ceramic is on my list.  Since I didn't have 4th gear when running the 1/4, the time was 13.9 @ 81mph.  All that tells me is the car will probably end up in the mid 12's if I can get the clutch to hold.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.goldengatefieros.org/RDosdorian_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||LT1 Chop Top||06-22-2001||03:22 PM||||I have to agree on the incredible power of the LT1 conversion....it is a lot to handle.  I have learned to NOT accellerate into tight corners or on wet streets.  This is the first V8 ANYTHING that I have owned, and I can say that I feel more comfortable going 80+ on my 800cc motorcycle than I do the LT1 just for the fact that in NO time the LT1 could be going 130mph, and the bike couldn't.  However, under normal driving, it is fantastic, very responsive, more power than you could imagine when you are used to the 2.8 V6. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LT1_Chop_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||FieroLT1||06-22-2001||03:38 PM||||You tell'em bro!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Winston Ojeda<BR><B>88 Formula LT1</B><BR>Milwaukee, Wisconsin, USA<BR><A HREF="http://www.FieroLT1.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.FieroLT1.com</A><BR><I>Home of the Fiero LT1</I><BR>  <A HREF="mailto:wojeda@FieroLT1.com">wojeda@FieroLT1.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000022||Hartz||06-22-2001||04:24 PM||||Ray - outstanding post.  That should be required reading for anyone considering a swap, regardless of engine choice.  I couldn't have summarized it better. <P>I'm still not sure you can have too much horsepower, but I'm getting closer!<P>I, personally, like the rumble and noise.  It adds to the "character" of the car.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  But people need to consider things like that when they're deciding what to build.  What do you want when you're done, and how are you going to use it?<P>Bummer about your clutch.  I'm making quite a bit less than 300 HP with my TPI setup and pulling 13.3, so I am quite sure 12's are in your future if you can make it stick!<P>Best of luck,<BR>Hartz<P>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||14
Z||000023||V8Fiero||06-22-2001||11:50 PM||||Well, I recently drove a fiero with a 3.4 that was done by Ed Parks from the fiero factory in Alabama. WOW! Couldnt believe the power this thing had. Granted the torque wasnt near a V8, but damn the power to weight ratio was excellent. On a road course could probably keep up if not beat a V8 fiero (just for the fact of the sloppiness of the V8,s in corners). Just my opinion. <P>I think this is going to be my next project.<BR>3.4 baby!<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000024||87FieroGTx||06-23-2001||12:10 AM|||| Will the 2.8 Turbo from IRM fit the 3.4? <P> If so you could git 300hp out of it no prob it should work good with the manual trans seeing the HP comes in at mid to high RPM  and good handling to boot.||||reg||1
Z||000025||TRiAD||06-23-2001||08:07 AM||||I'm planning an LT1 conversion in the '88 I'm buying. <P>The car will run on 17s, and have the complete Rayne/Held tubular suspension and coil-overs. Big brakes, too, probably of my own design. (I haven't seen anything else I'd be happy with). <P>I'll need to drive it every day, and will most likely autocross and do some open track work with it. <P>Archie is going to do the swap, and I'll probably install the rest myself. <P>Any of the LT1 guys have any input on whether this sounds like I'm going in the right direction (equipment wise) for what I'm wanting to do with the car?<P>Oh, BTW, if I ever race it in a straight line, it would be short distances at 80-90%, never all-out. <P>Thanks!<P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000026||88formula||06-23-2001||10:27 AM||||WOW! Excellent information! Really good thread! There are some intelligent people here.<P>I don’t have any experience with the v8 swap so I can’t say much about it other than I have seen them up close and personal. As for the v6 though I can say something. The 2.8/3.1/3.4-liter v6 engines are I think under appreciated and under estimated by many people. These engines can make some serious power for way under $10k. They respond well to the traditional engine modifications and are compact and lightweight; these are things to consider when you have a good handling car. The GM 60-degree engine has even been used with success in professional racing.<P>Someone said they don’t understand the point of a bigger v6. I guess it depends on what you are looking for but the point is that with more displacement you get more power. As one guy above said who owns a v8 fiero, the 3.4-liter v6 makes some impressive power. I also think the turbocharger is one of the best most reliable ways to increase the power of a small displacement engine. It’s not unreasonable to get sub six second 0-60 times from a v6 fiero. With a turbocharger and highly modified v6 you could get even lower. That to me is more than enough power to drive around with on the street. With the 3.4-liter v6 many are getting 0-60 mph times in 6-6.5 seconds with a nearly stock engine. <P>I agree with what many of you said above. When you modify any engine or car there will be some issues and you have to include these issues in your budget. You can’t expect a 300 horsepower v8 in a car that was designed for 140 horsepower to be as reliable. That would be an unreal expectation. <P>One more thing, I drove a fiero with a supercharged 3800 v6 engine in it with the five-speed transmission. The torque and power that engine makes is very impressive. It’s also a very reliable engine when you use the transmission that was designed for it to. Just ask Phil whom had his car featured in Hot Rod magazine.         <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000027||BV MotorSports||06-23-2001||04:55 PM||||I agree 88, there have been some really good points brought up in this thread that should help anyone looking for "more" outta their Fiero.<P>You see the only thing about mod'ing the 2.8-3.4 is how much you spend per HP/Torque gained.<BR>This is what got me started on the 3.8. I too had big plans for the 2.8 in my Fiero back in the days.<BR>I broke out my credit card and went to town. For the amount of $$$ I spent on hi-po parts, rebuild and custom stuff I had WAY more $$$ invested than I had planned. Yeah, I could of skimped here and there but, I just dont work that way. Anyway I had over 2k into my 2.8 (now 3.2) and wasnt happy with the hp/torque numbers the lil engine that could, made.<BR>I sold the whole thing to a "buddy" and bought a 3.8. <BR>So I know have 65 more hp and torque (over the 2.8) in a bone stock engine. Picked up the complete series II for $500 and have about another $300 in the custom parts needed for the swap.<BR>I figure 1k sounds like a reasonable figure for a 3.8 swap.<P>Now figure how much it would cost to get 65 more hp/torque out of the 2.8.<BR>Turbo....3k<BR>N20.......$600-800<BR>Stroker.....1,500-up (with rebuild)<P>These are just basic figures, Dennis L made a turbo kit for less than 1k. It just makes sense to me to go for a 3.8 vrs "hot rodding" a 2.8. And the great part is, the 3.8 is stock so there is always room for more power! As a matter of fact I am working on a Turbo system for my 3.8.<BR>Should be fun!<BR>Anyway, its your money and your car...spend wisely.<P>Steven<P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 89mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||1
Z||000028||BBBAD GT||06-26-2001||09:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RDosdorian:<BR><B>Slammed....Archie is waiting for me to pull the clutch to see what happend.  I am looking into other clutch options.  Ceramic is on my list.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>From what Chris told me when he had the hot 400+ hp 350 in my Fiero, the stock Archie (Centerforce DF) clutch won't hold at high rpms.  He was spinning it pretty bad, and even sent the whole thing to centerforce for an inspection.  Their response?  "its too much HP for this clutch, but the clutch is fine, we can upgrade it for $150 and it'll take the power"  Well, he swapped in a mild roller 350 (207 rwhp with no tuning) with the same clutch and its holding fine.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">        <P><A HREF="http://home.att.net/~nomercy5.0/V8Specs.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8 For Sale</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
